---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SOCIAL } from "@/config/site-config";

const effectiveDate = { iso: "2026-10-22", label: "October 22, 2026" };
---

<Layout
	title="SPS Terms of Service"
	ogImage="images/social-preview.png"
	description="Terms for using the Self Prompting System (SPS), a private Discord application.">
	<article>
		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">cat ~/sps/TERMS</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-file-earmark-text"></i> SPS Terms of Service
			</h2>
			<p>
				These terms cover use of the <b>Self Prompting System</b> ("SPS") Discord application, a private, experimental
				tool. In these terms, "the operator" means the person who runs SPS and is responsible for it, and "authorized
				users" means the people the operator has explicitly allowed to use its Discord interface.
			</p>
			<p>
				These terms don't cover this website; for that, see{" "}
				<a href="/notes/2026-03-12-licensing/">Licensing &amp; Disclaimer</a>.
			</p>
			<p>
				Effective <time datetime={effectiveDate.iso}>{effectiveDate.label}</time>. Read them alongside the{" "}
				<a href="/sps/privacy/">SPS Privacy Policy</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps --about</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-terminal"></i> What SPS is
			</h2>
			<p>
				SPS is a Windows-first orchestration system that runs locally and carries out engineering work through Claude
				Code CLI and Codex CLI workflows. Its Discord interface is private and experimental, and is configured for one
				operator, one private server, and one channel.
			</p>
			<p>
				Status notices are live: SPS posts private progress notices about project and run state. Feedback, run control,
				candidate review, and export are operator controls. When operator controls are enabled, authorized users can use
				them through slash commands and review buttons.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps auth --scope</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-person-check"></i> Who may use it
			</h2>
			<p>
				Only authorized users. Authorization covers the projects and scope the operator has approved, and nothing beyond
				them. Do not use SPS outside that scope or try to get around the limits on who can use it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps verify --trust-nothing</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-robot"></i> AI-generated output
			</h2>
			<p>
				Work produced through SPS, and the status summaries it reports, may be generated by AI tools. They can be
				incomplete or wrong. Verify anything that matters before relying on it; operator review is still required.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps review --accept</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-patch-check"></i> Reviews and acceptance
			</h2>
			<p>
				Accepting a review candidate is an authenticated decision tied to a specific revision, and it applies only to
				that revision. Independent engineering results can support follow-up work, but they do not complete a goal on
				their own.
			</p>
			<p>
				Functional tests of SPS, and the publication of these terms, are not the operator's acceptance of any
				engineering delivery.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps approve --physical</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-exclamation-triangle"></i> Approvals don't replace physical steps
			</h2>
			<p>
				Remote approval and physical readiness are separate. Approving something in Discord does not satisfy a step that
				requires a hardware connection, a physical action, or an observation. Those steps still need the evidence they
				specify.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">sps uptime</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-hourglass-split"></i> Access and availability
			</h2>
			<p>
				The operator may suspend or revoke access for misuse, security concerns, or operational reasons. SPS is
				experimental, and there is no guarantee that it will be available or run without interruption.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">ls ~/sps/third-party/</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-link-45deg"></i> Other terms that apply
			</h2>
			<p>
				Using SPS through Discord also means Discord's terms apply, as do the terms of the model providers involved:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://discord.com/terms" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
						Discord Terms of Service
					</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a
						href="https://support-dev.discord.com/hc/en-us/articles/8563934450327-Discord-Developer-Policy"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer">
						Discord Developer Policy
					</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/consumer-terms" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
						Anthropic Consumer Terms of Service
					</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://openai.com/policies/terms-of-use/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
						OpenAI Terms of Use
					</a>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="mb-10">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-success font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">git log -- ~/sps/TERMS</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<h2>
				<i class="icon-calendar-event"></i> Changes and contact
			</h2>
			<p>
				Material changes will be reflected on this page with an updated date, and authorized users will also be notified
				when these terms change.
			</p>
			<p>
				Questions about these terms can be sent to the operator at <a href={`mailto:${SOCIAL.email}`}>{SOCIAL.email}</a>
				. Last updated <time datetime={effectiveDate.iso}>{effectiveDate.label}</time>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="my-8 border-base-300" />

		<section class="mb-6">
			<p class="text-xs text-neutral-content mb-2">
				<span class="text-error font-bold">$</span>
				<span class="ml-1 text-warning">exit</span>
				<span class="blink" aria-hidden="true">
					_
				</span>
			</p>
			<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 mt-4">
				<a href="/sps/privacy/" class="link-action no-underline">
					<i class="icon-shield-check"></i>
					SPS Privacy
				</a>
				<a href="/notes/2026-03-12-licensing/" class="link-action no-underline">
					<i class="icon-info-circle"></i>
					Licensing
				</a>
			</div>
		</section>
	</article>
</Layout>
